Show the student an interactive step-by-step explanation: a diagram that stays in place and a panel that walks through one change per step. Use it when a mechanism, process, decomposition or relationship is easier to follow as a sequence than as text, and at most once per reply.

A separate writer builds the explanation from your brief. It sees neither this conversation nor the course material, so the brief carries everything it needs: what the student should understand, the student's misconception if there is one, the formulas, symbols and terms exactly as the course material writes them, the numbers to use (marked as from the course or illustrative), and the course documents it draws on with title and page or section.

Still answer in text, in one or two sentences with your usual [n] citations: what the explanation shows, and one question that has the student predict something or try a slider. If the explanation cannot be built, explain in text instead.
